= Human Capital Formation: People as Resource

A field lies idle and everyone calls it waste; a child lies idle and somehow no one does. Human capital formation is the remedy for that blindness — the process of equipping people with knowledge, health and skills so that they BECOME productive resources, not just mouths to feed.

- *How people become resource — the sources of human capital formation:*
  - *Expenditure on education:* the single largest source — schooling, colleges, technical institutes.
  - *Expenditure on health:* a healthy worker works more days, learns faster, retires later.
  - *On-the-job training:* firm-sponsored skill building — apprenticeships, in-house courses.
  - *Study programmes and migration:* even the cost of migrating for a job is an INVESTMENT in human capital — people move to where their skill pays best.
  - *Expenditure on information:* job search and market information help workers match to the right jobs.
- *The role of human capital in development:*
  - Skilled people raise PRODUCTIVITY directly — one trained operator does the work of three unskilled ones.
  - Innovation and adaptability — modern economies run on knowledge, not muscle.
  - Growth with EQUITY — education spreads earning power across society, unlike capital that concentrates.
  - Human capital pays DOUBLE: the private return (your salary) and the SOCIAL return (a more literate, healthier society) both rise.
- *Human capital vs physical capital — the classic contrast:*
  - Physical capital is SEPARABLE — you can sell your machine. Human capital is INSEPARABLE — knowledge lives in its owner.
  - Physical capital is built by investment alone; human capital uses BOTH consumption and investment spending (food is consumption AND health investment).
- *Growth of the education sector in India:* literacy climbed from about 18% (1951) past 74%; elementary schools, universities and technical institutes multiplied; flagship pushes (universalising elementary education, mid-day meals) pulled children into classrooms.
- *The problems that remain:* spending below real needs, teacher vacancies, weak learning outcomes despite enrolment, and EDUCATED unemployment — degrees without matching jobs. Quantity ran ahead of quality.

= Rural Development: Credit, Marketing and Cooperatives

Two-thirds of India still lives off villages — yet rural credit and marketing chains were built for exploitation, not support. Fixing both is rural development's heart.

- *Why farmers need credit:* inputs (seeds, fertiliser) bought before harvest, income arriving after — a months-long gap; plus land improvement and family consumption between harvests.
- *Sources of rural credit:*
  - *INSTITUTIONAL (the good side):* cooperative credit societies, commercial banks, regional rural banks (RRBs), government schemes, land development banks, self-help groups (SHGs).
  - *NON-INSTITUTIONAL (the trap side):* moneylenders, landlords, traders and relatives — instant but costly, often bonded to the crop, and exploitative.
  - *The reform story:* institutional agencies' share of rural credit rose sharply after bank nationalisation (1969) — yet the moneylender still survives where paperwork and collateral block the poor.
- *Problems of institutional credit:* tedious paperwork, collateral demands, delays, and concentration on big farmers — the small farmer's exclusion is the standing challenge.
- *Agricultural marketing — what farmers face:*
  - Too many INTERMEDIARIES between farm and fork — the farmer's share shrinks at every hop.
  - Faulty weighing, no grading, poor STORAGE (distress sales right after harvest at low prices), thin roads to markets.
- *The measures that fix marketing:*
  - *Regulated markets (APMC-style):* rules against malpractice, standard weights, oversight.
  - *Minimum Support Price (MSP):* a government guarantee — the floor under farm prices.
  - *Cooperative marketing:* farmers pool produce and bargain together (dairy cooperatives are the flagship success).
  - *Infrastructure:* warehousing, cold chains, roads, grading and standardisation facilities.

= Diversification and Organic Farming

Depending on one crop in one season is a gamble; diversification is insurance plus income.

- *Three diversification lanes:*
  - *Crop diversification:* shift towards high-value crops — fruits, vegetables, flowers — instead of only grain.
  - *Allied activities:* dairy, poultry, fisheries, horticulture, agro-forestry — income streams that don't wait for monsoon.
  - *Non-farm employment:* food processing, rural tourism, IT-enabled services in villages — jobs that keep youth in villages instead of crowding cities.
- *Organic farming — the alternative farming model:*
  - *What it is:* cultivation WITHOUT chemical fertilisers and pesticides — using cow dung manure, compost, bio-inputs.
  - *Why it matters:* eco-friendly soil and water, cheaper inputs long-run, PREMIUM prices and export demand (health-conscious buyers), food safety.
  - *The challenges:* lower yields in the transition years, certification cost and paperwork, shorter shelf life without preservatives, and limited initial marketing reach — organic needs organised supply chains.
  - *Where India shines:* traditional knowledge and low chemical use by default in many regions — the opportunity is to CERTIFY and market what farmers already practice.

= Employment: The Formal-Informal Divide

Employment is not one market — it is a stack of very different worlds. Understanding who does what, and on what terms, is the syllabus's employment core.

- *The three types of workers:*
  - *Self-employed:* run their own farm or shop — the LARGEST share of India's workforce.
  - *Regular salaried:* fixed wages, contracts, security — the FEWEST but best paid.
  - *Casual wage labour:* daily rates, no security, no promise of tomorrow's work — the most vulnerable.
- *Formal vs informal — the dividing tests:*
  - *Formal:* ten or more workers typically, WRITTEN contract, regular salary, benefits (pension, leave, provident fund) — in government and big registered firms.
  - *Informal:* no written contract, no benefits, no security — small firms, household enterprises, casual farms. MOST Indian workers live here.
  - *The divide cuts everywhere:* even formal-sector premises employ informal workers at the gate.
- *Workforce participation:* WPR counts workers per 1,000 people; it varies sharply by region, age and gender — women's participation rates lag far behind men's, in both rural and urban India.
- *Growth and its employment puzzle:* output growth has outpaced employment growth — the "jobless growth" concern. Services grew fast without hiring proportionately; agriculture still employs more people than it needs (disguised unemployment).
- *The policies:*
  - *MGNREGA (2005):* the Right to Work — 100 days of guaranteed wage employment per rural household per year, government-funded public works.
  - *Self-employment and skill programmes:* credit-plus-training schemes pushing youth into micro-enterprise and employable skills.

= Environment and Sustainable Development

The environment is not scenery around the economy — it IS the economy's foundation: the source of every input and the sink for every waste.

- *The four functions of the environment:*
  - *Supplies resources:* air, water, soil, minerals, forests — every input begins here.
  - *Absorbs waste (the SINK):* factories and households dump by-products — the environment digests them.
  - *Sustains life:* biodiversity, climate regulation, the oxygen-water cycles.
  - *Enhances quality of life:* rivers, scenery, open space — enjoyment beyond consumption.
- *Carrying capacity — the limit:* resource extraction beyond renewal rates and waste beyond absorption rates BREACH the carrying capacity. Two kinds of crisis follow:
  - *Extinction of resources:* renewable resources (forests, groundwater) used faster than they regenerate; non-renewables (coal, oil) simply shrink.
  - *The sink overflows:* pollution beyond nature's absorption — smog, dead rivers, toxic soil.
- *The state of India's environment:* city air thick with particulates, rivers contaminated by sewage and effluent, deforestation for land and timber, soil erosion and falling water tables — growth's unplanned bill arriving early.
- *Sustainable development — the definition that anchors answers:* development that meets the needs of the PRESENT generation without compromising the ability of FUTURE generations to meet their own needs (the Brundtland idea).
- *Strategies for sustainable development — match each to its problem:*
  - *Solar and wind power:* renewable energy replacing coal-fired electricity.
  - *CNG and LPG:* cleaner fuels for vehicles and kitchens — Delhi's air is the case study.
  - *Bio-composting and organic methods:* farm waste back to soil instead of chemical overload.
  - *Mini-hydel projects:* small-scale power from streams — local, low-damage.
  - *Traditional knowledge and practices:* water harvesting, indigenous crops — sustainable by design, rediscovered.

