= Practice Questions (Exam Pattern)

Ten questions in the CUET UG style. Attempt all of them — the paper gives $+5$ for each correct answer and takes nothing away for a wrong one.

#mcq(1, [Which of the following is the LARGEST source of human capital formation in India?], (
  [expenditure on health], [expenditure on education], [expenditure on migration], [expenditure on information],
))

#mcq(2, [Human capital differs from physical capital because human capital:], (
  [can be sold separately from its owner], [cannot be separated from its owner], [depreciates faster], [is always cheaper],
))

#mcq(3, [Which of these is a NON-institutional source of rural credit?], (
  [regional rural banks], [cooperative credit societies], [moneylenders], [self-help groups],
))

#mcq(4, [Regulated agricultural markets (APMC-type) were set up mainly to:], (
  [raise export duties], [protect farmers from malpractice in sale], [fix crop patterns], [subsidise fertilisers],
))

#mcq(5, [An advantage of organic farming is that it:], (
  [guarantees higher yields immediately], [avoids chemical inputs and is eco-friendly], [needs no certification], [increases shelf life of produce],
))

#mcq(6, [Workers with NO written contract, no job security and no benefits belong to the:], (
  [formal sector], [informal sector], [public sector], [organised sector],
))

#mcq(7, [The largest share of India's workforce consists of:], (
  [regular salaried workers], [casual wage workers], [self-employed workers], [unpaid family helpers],
))

#mcq(8, [MGNREGA (2005) guarantees:], (
  [free education to all children], [100 days of wage employment per rural household], [pensions to the elderly], [subsidised food grains],
))

#mcq(9, [The "sink" function of the environment refers to its role in:], (
  [supplying minerals], [absorbing waste], [providing scenery], [regulating birth rates],
))

#mcq(10, [Sustainable development is development that:], (
  [maximises present consumption], [stops economic growth], [meets present needs without harming future generations' ability to meet theirs], [focuses only on industrial growth],
))

= Detailed Solutions

Each solution shows the working, not just the verdict — cover the answer line, attempt the reasoning, then compare.

#solution(1, [Education spending is the biggest human-capital investment — schools and colleges convert children into skilled workers. Health and training follow; migration and information are smaller streams.], answer: [B])

#solution(2, [Knowledge lives INSIDE its owner — you cannot sell your skill apart from yourself. Machines, buildings and shares are separable; human capital is not.], answer: [B])

#solution(3, [Banks, cooperatives, RRBs and SHGs are institutional agencies. Moneylenders operate outside any regulatory system — the classic non-institutional (and exploitative) source.], answer: [C])

#solution(4, [Regulated markets standardise weights, curb malpractice and bring oversight — protecting farmers from the cheating and distress that unregulated sales invited.], answer: [B])

#solution(5, [Organic farming's core benefit is chemical-free, eco-friendly cultivation with premium markets. Yields often FALL initially and certification is a hurdle — the other options claim the opposite.], answer: [B])

#solution(6, [No contract, no security, no benefits — the informal sector's signature. Formal work (public or organised private) carries all three.], answer: [B])

#solution(7, [Self-employment — own-account farms and enterprises — employs the largest share of Indian workers; regular salaried employment is the smallest slice.], answer: [C])

#solution(8, [MGNREGA is the Right to Work law: any rural household demanding work gets up to 100 days of government-funded wage employment a year.], answer: [B])

#solution(9, [The environment digests society's waste — effluents, emissions, garbage. That absorbing role is the SINK function; overload it and pollution builds.], answer: [B])

#solution(10, [The Brundtland definition balances generations: meet today's needs while preserving tomorrow's capacity to meet its own — growth within limits, not growth denied.], answer: [C])

